Z||000000||AnimalGT||12-12-2001||12:44 AM||||I'm going to play around with a few fiberglass projects this winter, and I need to know how critical it is for the resin to cure at specific temps.  I have to work in a garage heated only w/ a space heater.  It would cost too much to heat the garage to ~70* and keep it there for long enough for the resin to cure.  So, my question is: can resin cure reliably at lower temps (above freezing at least)?  Some of the things I'm making will need to be able to withstand some loading, and I don't want them to fall apart in 6 months because of improper curing (front battery box & front hood vent for example). <BR>Scott  <p>[This message has been edited by AnimalGT (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||5
